21.3.m:
"I settle thee in the seat of life," [he says];
he places life itself as highest;
therefore life is the highest of the breaths.
There is a naturally perforated [brick],
for the emission of the vital breaths,
for the sighting of the heavenly world.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati;
when he makes the horse sniff [the altar],
he piles him with Prajāpati himself.
Then this Vikarṇī [brick];
this Vikarṇī is indeed the stride of the gods;
because it is the Vikarṇī,
he makes him step out according to the stride of the gods.
[It is accompanied by] one having Vāyu (wind);
breath is Vāyu; the naturally perforated [brick] is life;
life and breath he places in him together.
He kindles him indeed in that he piles [the fire];
he makes it flare up by means of that which has Vāyu.
Having carried [the fire in the pan] which is the head of the year, he should pile the fire.
Agni Vaiśvānara is indeed the year;
his bricks are the days and nights;
he piles him having attained the bricks, he prospers.
If he were not to pile [it] thus, he would deprive him of his dear body;
since he piles [it] thus,
he enriches him with his dear body;
Vaiśvānara is piled here possessing his body.
He lays down the seasonal [bricks],
for the support of the seasons.
He lays them down two by two,
for the seasons are two by two.
He lays down four in the middle layer;
the middle layer is, as it were, the atmosphere;
the atmosphere is, as it were, loose;
there are four quarters;
he firmly establishes himself in the quarters.
The gods fashioned them from the seasons;
that is the nature of the seasonal [bricks] as seasonal;
he who, knowing thus, lays them down firmly establishes himself in season after season as it arrives.
They have different beginnings and identical conclusions;
therefore the single year has different forms.
Having laid down an avakā plant, he lays down the brick;
the avakā is indeed the womb of Agni Vaiśvānara;
he lays them down in their own womb,
for appeasement, that there be no burning.
He who, knowing thus, lays them down prospers.
Now these Viśvajyotis [bricks]:
these are indeed the breath-supporters of the sacrificer;
he holds breath by the first, circulating breath by the second, downward breath by the third.
These deities are belonging to the heavenly world;
by them he goes to the heavenly world.
These deities are luminous;
Agni becomes his light in this world, Vāyu in the atmosphere, Sūrya in heaven;
all these worlds become luminous for him.
They have [formulas mentioning] overlords;
by them he attains overlordship.
"Fill the space, fill the gap," [he says];
whatever of it is deficient, whatever is a gap, that he fills up.
"Then sit firm," [he says];
he strengthens it.
"Indra and Agni, Bṛhaspati [have settled thee]," [he says];
Indra and Agni are vigor and virility;
with vigor and virility he piles him.
"Those milk-yielding cows of his," [he says];
therefore there is marrow in every joint.
"The dappled ones mix the Soma," [he says];
the dappled one is food;
he secures food.
"In the three bright realms of heaven," [he says];
[there are] three pressings;
he piles him in the pressings.
The anuṣṭubh is the following [meter];
the anuṣṭubh is breath;
therefore breath moves along through all the limbs.
The anuṣṭubh is speech;
whatever of his here is not done, whatever is not piled, that he arranges by speech, that he heals.

21.5.m:
Through the consecration, the virāj is to be obtained.
He should be consecrated for three nights;
the virāj is three-footed;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for six nights;
the seasons are six, [such is] the year;
the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for ten nights;
the virāj has ten syllables;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for twelve nights;
twelve months are the year; the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for thirteen nights;
thirteen months are the year; the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for fifteen nights;
fifteen are the nights of a half-month; half-month by half-month the year is obtained;
the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for twenty-four nights;
twenty-four are the half-months of the year; half-month by half-month the year is obtained;
the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for a month;
that which is a month is the year;
the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for four months;
for four months indeed the Vasus bore him;
they conquered the earth [and] the gāyatrī metre;
for eight [months] the Rudras [bore him];
they conquered the intermediate space [and] the triṣṭubh metre;
for twelve [months] the Ādityas [bore him];
they conquered heaven [and] the jagatī metre;
thence indeed they went to distinction, to pre-eminence among the gods.
Therefore, having borne [the fire in the pan] for twelve months, he should pile the fire [altar];
twelve months are the year; the year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
his bricks are the days and nights;
with bricks obtained he piles him;
he prospers;
he indeed goes to distinction, to pre-eminence among his equals.
He sings the Gāyatra [sāman] in front;
the Gāyatra is indeed the head of the gods; Agni is of gāyatrī nature;
therefore he sings the Gāyatra in front,
on a [verse] addressed to Agni Pavamāna.
This one who purifies himself is indeed the breath;
Agni is the mouth of the gods;
he places the breath at the mouth itself;
therefore the breath is at the mouth.
He sings the Rathantara on the north, the Bṛhat on the south;
he lays down his wings indeed.
This [earth] is the Rathantara; that [heaven] is the Bṛhat;
by these two he encloses him.
He sings the Yajñāyajñīya at the tail, the Vāmadevya at the hip;
the Yajñāyajñīya is the self; the Vāmadevya is the body;
with a self, with a body, he piles him.
The heart is near the armpit;
therefore the heart is near the armpit; it is without a verse;
therefore the heart is boneless.
He sings around [him] with the Arkya [verses];
Agni is the arka;
he sings around the arka with the Arkya [verses],
for appeasement.

21.6.m:
Between darkness and the heavenly world is indeed the year.
Luminous are these thousand-bearing bricks.
When he sprinkles [them] with gold chips,
with light he illuminates the year for him.
"Thou art the measure of a thousand, thou art the image of a thousand," [he says];
for these worlds are commensurate with a thousand.
They strike at Agni in that which is not done here for him, which is not piled.
Gold is immortality.
When he sprinkles with gold chips,
he unites his body with immortality.
"May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni," [he says];
he makes them milch-cows;
they wait upon him yielding his wishes in yonder world.
The gods indeed excluded Rudra from the sacrifice;
he turned round upon the gods with drawn [bow];
those gods saw this Śatarudrīya;
with it they appeased him.
When he offers the Śatarudrīya,
with it he appeases him.
First he offers at knee-height;
he sacrifices away Rudra from this [earth];
then at navel-height;
he sacrifices away Rudra from the intermediate space;
then at a man's height;
he sacrifices away Rudra from that [heaven].
From here upwards he sacrifices away Rudra;
then at a man's height;
then at navel-height;
then at knee-height;
from yonder hitherward he sacrifices away Rudra.
That amounts to six;
six are the seasons;
from the seasons he sacrifices away Rudra.
He offers with gavīdhukā groats, or with wild sesame, or with butter of a kusaya, or with the milk of a wild beast.
If he were to offer with domestic [produce], he would make Rudra frequent the village;
with wild [produce] he sacrifices away Rudra towards the wilderness.
He offers with an arka-leaf;
Agni is the arka;
with the arka he sacrifices him away from the arka.
On the last brick of the northern wing he offers;
this is indeed the direction of Rudra;
having brought [it] back to his own direction, he sacrifices [him] away.
Agni is a beast;
now indeed he is born, when he is piled;
just as a new-born calf desires to reach the udder, so he now desires to reach his share;
he meditates upon both the sacrificer and the Adhvaryu.
When he offers the Śatarudrīya,
with his share he appeases him.
The Aṅgirases, going to the heavenly world, poured out the cauldron on a she-goat;
suffering heat, she touched a leaf;
that became the arka plant;
that is the arka-nature of the arka.
When he offers with an arka-leaf,
[it is] for possessing the same origin.
Whom he hates, in the track of his cattle he should throw [it] down;
whichever beast steps [on it] first, they incur misfortune.
Those [formulas] which are last, those he should make the sacrificer recite;
these deities indeed belong to the heavenly world;
with them he goes to the heavenly world.
These deities are indeed suitable for sorcery;
whom he hates, of him he should say: "I place that one in your jaws";
from these deities he tears him away;
instantly he perishes.

21.7.m:
With 'The vigour in the stone, resting in the mountain,' he sprinkles around with water.
Water is the sap of Prajāpati.
With the sap of Prajāpati he gratifies it.
Gratified, pleased, not hungering, it waits upon him.
'In the stone be thy hunger; let thy heat fall upon him whom we hate,' [he says].
Whomsoever he hates, him he afflicts with its hunger and its heat.
Three times he sprinkles around;
three are these worlds;
from these worlds he appeases it.
Three times he goes around again without sprinkling;
that amounts to six;
six indeed are the seasons;
from the seasons he appeases it.
If he should practice sorcery, having gone around three times again without sprinkling, he should crush [the pot] on the right buttock, [saying,] 'Here do I crush so-and-so, descendant of so-and-so, son of so-and-so.'
Taking whatever heat is in the fire, he strikes him with it;
as it breaks, he undergoes misfortune.
In that he mentions his name,
he cuts off his very root.
When piled, this [fire] is the wrath of Varuṇa.
He drags across with a frog;
he passes away with the lifespan of a frog;
he harms neither domestic animals nor wild [animals].
Therefore does one come to it in spring and in the rainy season,
for it is affected by heat.
He drags across with avakā and with a cane-branch.
Avakā is the cream of the waters; cane is the flower of the waters.
Waters are appeased;
with the appeased ones he appeases its heat.
With three [verses];
threefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, so great does he appease his heat.
Prajāpati created offspring;
they hungered;
to them he gave food;
they said, 'It has come to us.'
Food is purifying.
In that they contain [the word] 'purifying',
[it is] for the eating of food.
Pleasant does Agni become for him who has piled [the altar].
He causes it to be dragged across with seven;
seven indeed are the metres;
with the metres he appeases its heat.
He drags across with eight;
eight-syllabled is the gāyatrī;
Agni is of the gāyatrī [nature];
as great as Agni is, so great does he appease his heat.
Having swept it together, he throws it upon the rubbish-heap;
this indeed is its heat;
in heat he places its heat.
Against whomsoever he bears hatred, at him he should throw it;
with heat he strikes him.
In ghee taken five times, he throws five chips of gold.
Gold is brilliance, ghee is lifespan;
with brilliance he sprinkles over its lifespan.
Moreover, with a fivefold offering he grasps Agni.
'Homage to thy wrath, to thy heat,' [saying this] he steps onto [it];
for having paid homage, they approach a superior.
He sprinkles crosswise,
for non-sameness.
'To the one seated among men, veṭ,' [he says],
for firm standing.
Some gods are eaters of oblations, others are non-eaters of oblations;
let the fire-piler eat, he secures both of these;
since he both sacrifices and sprinkles down,
he pleases them, he secures them; both become pleased with him at the sacrifice.
With a heavy fistful he sprinkles down;
for that belongs most to Prajāpati;
thereby for him it is neither offered nor unoffered.
He makes two shoes of black-antelope skin;
the black-antelope skin is the form of the brahman;
Agni is death;
by the brahman he conceals [himself] from death;
he conceals [himself] from death.
He should make either shoe apart from food-eating;
he conceals [himself] from death;
he secures food-eating.
He who piles the fire is deprived of vital breaths, of cattle.
He steps onto it first, [saying,] 'Giver of out-breath, giver of circulating breath';
with the vital breaths he furnishes him.
'Giver of splendour, giver of room,' [he says];
splendour is offspring, room is cattle;
with offspring and with cattle he furnishes him.
'Giver of out-breath, giver of in-breath,' [saying this] he steps down,
according to form;
with two [verses];
two-footed is the sacrificer;
for firm standing.

21.8.m:
Indra slew Vṛtra.
Slain, [Vṛtra] bound him with sixteen coils.
Having burnt away the coils of evil with Agni, he freed himself from evil by means of the two Vaiśvakarmana [verses].
When he offers with the [verse] addressed to Agni,
having burnt away the coils of evil with Agni, he frees himself from evil by means of the two Vaiśvakarmana [verses].
He should offer with [the verses] one by one if he should desire, 'May he be freed from evil slowly';
he is freed from evil slowly.
Having recited all at once, he should offer with the last if he should desire, 'May he be freed from evil swiftly';
he is freed swiftly from evil.
There are sixteen [verses];
man is of sixteen parts;
as great as his self is, so much of it does he free from evil.
Moreover, he had bound him with sixteen coils.
He offers with two different hymns,
for the different potency of the two hymns.
He places firewood [on the fire];
he sets apart a share for him;
just as one prepares provisions for someone who is coming, so is this hospitality made for Agni.
He places three [pieces of firewood];
for threefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, for him he prepares hospitality.
They are made of udumbara wood;
vigour is the udumbara;
he places vigour into him.
He anoints [them] with ghee;
this is the dear abode of Agni, namely ghee;
he prepares hospitality for his dear abode.
'Up may all the gods [bear] thee'—
the all-gods are the vital breaths;
by means of the vital breaths he lifts him up;
the all-gods are men;
by means of men he lifts him up.
'O Agni, may they bear [thee] with thoughts'—
for whatever thought he lifts him up, therewith he makes him prosper.
'The five directions'—
he leads him along the directions.
Two [verses] are enclosing,
for the smiting away of the rakṣases.
'The golden-haired, sun-rayed one'—
[it is a verse] addressed to Savitṛ,
for impulsion.
'The gods performing the sacrificial service stood for the gods'—
this is the milking of blessings.
'This measurer'—
this is the measurer of the heavenly world.
'The bull, the ocean, the ruddy, fair-winged'—with this he places a stone on the Āgnīdhra [hearth].
Without foundation, as it were, and without support is this intermediate space;
the Āgnīdhra is the intermediate space.
When he places [the stone] on the Āgnīdhra,
he provides a support.
'Placed in the midst of heaven, the dappled stone'—
the dappled [stone] is food;
he secures food.
With two [verses] he places [it];
two-footed is the sacrificer;
for stability.
With six [verses] they proceed from the Āgnīdhra;
six indeed are the seasons;
with the seasons indeed.
With four up to the tail;
four are the metres;
with the metres indeed.
'Indra'—
he secures indriya-power.
'They have magnified'—
he attains growth.
'The prizes'—
food is the prizes;
he secures food.
With an anuṣṭubh addressed to all the gods they proceed;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
speech belongs to all the gods;
all forms are this [anuṣṭubh];
all forms are made in the piled Agni;
therefore they proceed with this [verse].
'Invoking favour'—
the sacrifice is favour;
he secures the sacrifice.
'By the impulsion of the prize, with the elevation I have elevated [thee]'—
the elevation is the brahman, the depression is the brahman;
yonder sun rising is the elevation, setting it is the depression;
by the brahman he elevates himself, by the brahman he presses down the rival.
'Then may Indra and Agni cast my rivals apart in all directions'—
Indra and Agni are force and valour;
by force and valour he thrusts away the rival.

21.9.m:
'Stride ye to the firmament with Agni,' [he says];
this is the striding onto the heavenly world.
'Go forward along the eastern quarter, knowing,' [he says];
he turns towards the world of the gods.
'Shining, illumine all regions,' [he says];
therefore Agni shines upon all directions.
'From the earth I have mounted into the intermediate space, from the intermediate space I have mounted into heaven,' [he says];
these indeed are the steps to these worlds.
'Going to heaven, they looked not back,' [he says];
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
'O Agni, go forward first of those who long for the gods,' [he says];
he places sight both in the gods and in men.
'Desiring to sacrifice, in harmony with the Bhṛgus,' [he says];
he leads him to the heavenly world.
With five [verses] he steps upon [it];
fivefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, having paid homage to him, he steps upon [him].
With 'Night and dawn,' [and] 'O Agni, thousand-eyed,' two linked verses, he offers;
he joins together day and night;
therefore day and night are joined.
He offers [the milk] of a black cow with a white calf;
by means of the day he causes the night to be given to him.
Of various forms indeed are cattle, of various vows;
he formed them in this very gift.
He offers with milk;
recognising this, cattle wait upon him.
He offers with sour milk on the naturally perforated [brick];
yonder indeed is the naturally perforated [brick], vigour is sour milk;
in yonder [world] he places vigour;
therefore from yonder they live upon vigour [flowing] hitherward.
'A fair-winged eagle art thou,' with a gāyatrī [verse] he sets [it] down;
on this [earth] he causes him to step.
'Fill the intermediate space with light,' with a triṣṭubh [verse];
into the intermediate space he causes him to step.
'Invoked, fair-faced in the east,' with a jagatī [verse];
in yonder [world] he causes him to step.
Having made him united with these deities, he establishes him.
With three [verses];
threefold indeed is Agni;
as great as Agni is, to that establishment he leads him.
With '[That] of Savitṛ,' with a verse to Savitṛ, he places [a kindling-stick];
for impulsion.
One made of udumbara wood;
vigour indeed is udumbara;
vigour he attaches to him.
Either the fire-piler milks Agni, or Agni milks the fire-piler.
Kaṇva indeed knew the milking of this higher good;
as they milk a given cow, just so the fire-piler milks Agni with this.
'O Agni, may we pay worship to thee,' [he says];
for prosperity.
One made of vikaṅkata wood;
when Agni was created, his splendour went into the vikaṅkata;
that indeed he wins.
'Kindled, O Agni, shine before us,' [with this verse he places] one made of śamī wood;
for appeasement.
With a virāj [verse];
this indeed is an iron tube with ears.
Somadakṣa Kauśreya asked the sacrificial session-attendees about this: 'Do you know the iron tube with ears?'
'We know that which is in the forest,' [they said].
Cattle indeed are its ears;
he wins cattle.
This vajra, a hundred-slayer, the gods hurled down at the asuras with 'For so much, to thee!';
he hurls down a vajra at his rival.
Lay him low!
'Seven, O Agni, are thy kindling-sticks,' [he says];
by this he gratifies the sets of seven.
He offers with a full ladle;
as for a new-born calf one fastens the teat, just so for him he fastens this portion.
He offers with sour milk in front, with ghee from above;
vigour indeed is sour milk, fiery energy is ghee;
vigour and fiery energy, united, he places in him at the end;
moreover, he causes both of these to be given to him.

21.10.m:
Now this Apratiratha [hymn];
by this the gods conquered the asuras without resistance;
that is the Apratiratha's quality of being without resistance;
without resistance he conquers his rival with this.
This indeed is the Gāyatrī with wings of light;
by it he goes to the heavenly world.
It consists of ten verses;
the virāj has ten syllables, food is the virāj;
in the virāj, in food, he is established.
With this Bharadvāja went forth, arming Pratardana;
thence he became the kingdom.
Whom he should desire, 'May he be the kingdom,' him he should go forth arming with this;
he becomes the kingdom.
This is the oblation named 'Displacer of the world';
from post after post he thrusts his rival with this.
He should perform it in battle;
pointed or made of bāndhuka should the fuel be;
if the coals twist back, the army of the enemies twists back.
Now to Agni Vaiśvānara [an oblation] on twelve potsherds [is offered];
Agni Vaiśvānara is the year;
this is the dear body of the year, which is Vaiśvānara's;
when [it is offered] to Agni Vaiśvānara,
he shows hospitality to his dear body.
Vaiśvānara is the nobility, the Maruts are the commonalty;
when the [offerings] to the Maruts are offered after the Vaiśvānara,
he harnesses the commonalty to the nobility.
He recites the offering verse for the Vaiśvānara aloud, he offers the [offerings] to the Maruts silently;
therefore the nobility speaks over the commonalty.
Agni is cattle;
with Agni as their mouth Prajāpati created cattle;
the Maruts are cattle;
when the [offerings] to the Maruts are offered after the Vaiśvānara,
[it is] for the propagation of cattle.
If he should turn towards the east, he would be bewildered by the divine commonalty;
if towards the south, he would belong to the fathers;
if towards the west, he would belong to the rakṣases;
if towards the north, he would belong to men;
straight, firmly established, it must be offered;
for the establishment of the sacrificer.
Now these [offerings] to the Maruts
are on seven potsherds each;
for seven by seven are the Maruts, in sevenfold troops;
having recited troop after troop, he offers;
troop by troop he gratifies the Maruts.
With the troop that is to be recited in the forest, he offers in the middle;
this is the nobility of the Maruts, the others are the commonalty;
he places the nobility in the midst of the commonalty.
If he should desire, 'May I slay the nobility with the commonalty,' he should confuse with the others that troop which is to be recited in the forest;
with the commonalty he slays the nobility.
If he should desire, 'May I slay the commonalty with the nobility,' he should make the puronuvākyā and yājyā in triṣṭubh;
might and vigour are the triṣṭubh;
having taken away might and vigour from the commonalty, he binds it to the nobility.
Whom he should desire, 'May I thrust him from the nobility,' with that troop which is to be recited in the forest he should move the chariot-stand placed on the fire;
this indeed bears the nobility of the rājanya,
the nobility of the Maruts;
by the nobility he thrusts him from the nobility.
'Following the path of Indra, the divine clans, the Maruts,' [he says];
this indeed is the following path of the gods;
by the following path of the gods he makes a following path for the sacrificer.
He kindles him when he piles [the altar];
he inflames [him] with the names of the Maruts.

21.11.m:
He offers the shower of wealth (vasordhārā).
Verily, this is offered with the thought, 'May there be a shower of wealth for me.'
Verily, this stream of ghee attends upon the sacrificer in the other world.
He offers continuously,
for the continuity of proper food,
for non-interruption.
For him whom he hates, he should interrupt [the stream];
he cuts off his proper food.
He offers with ghee.
Ghee is brilliance;
the shower of wealth is brilliance;
he places brilliance in brilliance.
Verily, this is offered for the ordering of what is not ordered,
for the obtaining of what is not obtained,
for the overcoming of what is not overcome.
'Out-breath and in-breath,' [he says];
he places out-breath and in-breath in the sacrificer.
'Food and sight,' [he says];
this indeed is the form of food;
by form alone he obtains food.
He offers these twelve [times] twelve.
Twelve are the months, [such is] the year;
by means of the year alone he obtains proper food.
'Agni and water,' [he says];
this indeed is the womb of food;
he obtains food together with its womb.
He offers [formulas] half-dedicated to Indra,
for obtaining the deities.
In that he offers [formulas] half-dedicated to Indra,
therefore Indra has the greatest share among the deities.
He makes Indra the higher one;
he establishes the sacrificer in sensory power.
'Aṃśu and Raśmi,' [he says];
Aṃśu and Raśmi are the mouth of the sacrifice;
he obtains the mouth of the sacrifice.
'Spoons and cups,' [he says];
these indeed are the forms of the sacrifice;
by forms alone he obtains the sacrifice.
'The final bath (avabhṛtha) and the call of Svagā,' [he says];
[it is] for stability.
'Agni and the hot cauldron (gharma),' [he says];
this indeed is Agni's holy radiance (brahmavarcasa);
he obtains just that.
'A two-and-a-half-year-old steer and a two-and-a-half-year-old heifer,' [he says];
so many indeed are the ages of cattle;
he obtains cattle endowed with their ages.
'One and three,' [he says];
one and three are the meter of men;
he obtains the meter of men.
'Four and eight,' [he says];
four and eight are the meter of the gods;
he obtains the meter of the gods.
The even and uneven stomas are a pair,
for procreation.
He forms a pair by increasing by two;
he procreates by increasing by four.
He offers [with numbers] up to thirty-three, saying, 'One and three';
thirty-three are the deities;
he obtains the deities.
Up to forty-eight, saying, 'Four and eight';
the jagatī has forty-eight syllables;
cattle belong to the jagatī;
he obtains cattle.
In this world he thrives through the meter of men, in that [other world] through the meter of the gods;
he wins both worlds.
'Strength and propagation,' [he says];
these are thirteen sacred utterances; the year has thirteen months;
at the end he ascends the year.
'May life-span succeed through the sacrifice,' [he says];
this is the ordering of the sacrifice.
'Ṛc and sāman,' [he says];
the shower of wealth is proper food;
having obtained proper food through the shower of wealth, having become an eater of food,
'Stoma and yajus,' [he says];
stoma and yajus are divine rulership;
at the end he enters into divine rulership.

21.12.m:
The gods ran a race for the propagation of strength (vājaprasavya);
Agni won it.
When he offers the vājaprasavya [oblations],
[it is] for the winning of proper food.
He offers of each kind of food;
he obtains all food.
From whatever [food] he does not offer, he is deprived of that.
If he should not offer of something, he should sprinkle it on the kindling-wood;
thereby he obtains it.
He offers with an udumbara spoon;
the udumbara is vigor;
through vigor he obtains proper food.
This is the consecration of Agni;
therefore, one who has piled the fire should not run when it rains;
if he runs, he runs away from proper food.
Now, these are the kingdom-holders (rāṣṭrabhṛt);
he who is piled is the royal power among the fires;
these verily uphold his kingdom;
that is the kingdom-holders' nature as kingdom-holders;
through them he hands over the kingdom to him.
These are six;
six are the seasons;
by means of the seasons he hands over the kingdom to him;
moreover, he is established in the seasons.
The offerings are pairs,
for procreation.
Twelve [are they];
twelve are the months, [such is] the year;
by means of the year he hands over the kingdom to him;
moreover, he is established in the year.
He offers the thirteenth in the front of the chariot;
the chariot is a vajra;
by means of the vajra he conquers the directions;
having conquered the directions, he goes forth in his chariot.
He offers the wind-oblations;
he obtains the wind.
Verily, this [wind] blows upon the fire-piler in that world.
He offers thrice;
three are these worlds;
from these worlds he obtains the wind;
these worlds blow the wind for him.
He offers with hollowed hands;
Vāyu is [taken] by the portion of the hand;
one cannot reach Vāyu with [cut] portions;
all at once he reaches him.
'Thou art the ocean, misty,' [he says];
these are the forms of the wind;
by forms alone he obtains the wind.
The gāyatrī is the out-breath;
the Havirdhāna cart-shed belongs to the gāyatrī.
The triṣṭubh is the circulating breath;
the Āgnīdhra belongs to the triṣṭubh.
The jagatī is the in-breath;
the sadas belongs to the jagatī.
'Set in the middle of heaven, the dappled stone,' [he says];
that dappled stone is breath-of-life (asu);
he places breath-of-life between out-breath and in-breath,
for the separation of out-breath and in-breath.
He places twenty-one [bricks] at the Hotṛ's hearth;
the twenty-one-fold is the support of the stomas;
[it is] for stability.
Eleven at the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin's [hearth];
the triṣṭubh has eleven syllables;
the triṣṭubh is virility;
he obtains virility.
Eight in each of the others;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
he is established in the gāyatrī.
Six in the Mārjālīya;
six are the seasons;
the seasons carried this around to the south;
the seasons are the Fathers;
therefore six are placed here.

22.2.m:
Just as the re-establishing of the fire (punarādheya) is, so is the re-piling (punaściti).
He who does not prosper by the establishment of the fires sets up the re-establishing of the fire;
if one should not prosper by the piled fire, he should pile the re-piling;
he does indeed prosper.
The first layer is the self;
the second, offspring;
the third, grandson.
Piling the third fire, he should place these [bricks] in the middle layer,
for continuity.
This fire is called Napātka ('having a grandson');
he eats food who, knowing thus, places these.
He places eight of these;
eight syllables has the gāyatrī;
he establishes himself in the gāyatrī.
Whatever oblation the fire-piler offers on [a hearth] without bricks, that leaks away;
after it leaking away, the sacrifice leaks away;
after the sacrifice, the sacrificer.
When these are placed,
[it is] for the establishing of the oblations.
After them being established, the sacrifice is established;
after the sacrifice, the sacrificer.
And he places eight of them;
eight syllables has the gāyatrī;
Agni belongs to the gāyatrī;
as great as Agni is, so great he piles him.
Having placed these eight, he covers [them] over with loose earth;
eight syllables has the gāyatrī;
the gāyatrī is the brahman;
with the brahman he piles him.
With the gāyatrī metre, having placed eleven [bricks] with the Lokaṃpṛṇā verse, he covers [them] over with loose earth;
eleven syllables has the triṣṭubh;
the triṣṭubh is manly strength;
with manly strength he piles him.
With the triṣṭubh metre, having placed twelve with the Lokaṃpṛṇā verse, he covers [them] over with loose earth;
twelve syllables has the jagatī;
cattle belong to the jagatī;
with cattle he piles him.
With the jagatī metre [there are] three layers, three earth-fillings;
that amounts to six;
six are the seasons;
he establishes himself in the seasons.
Concerning this they say:
'This is the high altar; whatever former piled fire of his there may be, he should sacrifice having settled near to it.'
Just as he adds more to [a fire] already burning, in the same way he places fiery energy upon this prior [fire].
Concerning this they say:
'Who ought to awaken a superior who is fast asleep?'
When he sacrifices,
he strengthens him with the oblation.
When he piles the fire, he makes a house.
Whatever he gains elsewhere, he brings to his house.
Whether he sacrifices with a regular offering, or with an animal sacrifice, or with Soma, whatever former piled fire of his there may be, he should sacrifice having settled near to it;
just as having gained elsewhere one brings it to his house, exactly so is that.

22.3.m:
Having borne the [fire] in the pan (ukhya) for a year, when the second year has elapsed, he should offer an eight-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni, an eleven-pot-sherd [cake] to Indra, a twelve-pot-sherd [cake] to the All-Gods, a pap to Bṛhaspati, and a three-pot-sherd [cake] to Viṣṇu; when the third year has elapsed, he should sacrifice with the Abhijit [rite].
In that it is of eight pot-sherds,
eight syllables has the gāyatrī;
the morning pressing belongs to Agni and to the gāyatrī;
thereby he held fast the morning pressing and the gāyatrī metre.
In that it is of eleven pot-sherds,
eleven syllables has the triṣṭubh;
the midday pressing belongs to Indra and to the triṣṭubh;
thereby he held fast the midday pressing and the triṣṭubh metre.
In that it is of twelve pot-sherds,
twelve syllables has the jagatī;
the third pressing belongs to the All-Gods and to the jagatī;
thereby he held fast the third pressing and the jagatī metre.
In that it is to Bṛhaspati,
Bṛhaspati is the brahman;
thereby he held fast the brahman.
In that it is to Viṣṇu,
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
thereby he held fast the sacrifice.
In that he sacrifices with the Abhijit,
[it is] for conquest (abhijiti).
In that he bears the [fire] in the pan for a year,
thereby he wins this world.
In that he piles a second [fire],
thereby he wins the intermediate space.
In that he sacrifices,
thereby he wins that world yonder.
Para Āṭnāra, Kakṣīvant Auśija, and Trasadasyu Paurukutsa, desiring offspring, piled this [fire];
then they obtained a thousand sons each.
He extends; he goes to that abundance to which they went, who, knowing thus, piles this [fire].

22.4.m:
Prajāpati piled this fire in the beginning by means of the seasons, the year: with spring the front part, with summer the right wing, with the rains the left, with autumn the tail, with winter the middle.
With the priesthood (brahman) he piled its front part, with the nobility (kṣatra) the right wing, with the common people the left, with the beasts the tail, with food-desire the middle.
So much indeed is [the world];
as much as there is, that he wins.
They hear of him having piled the fire; he eats food, he shines.
'What is the first layer? What is the loose earth (purīṣa)?' say the theologians.
This [earth] is the first layer, the plants are the loose earth;
the atmosphere is the second, the birds are the loose earth;
the sky is the third, the constellations are the loose earth;
the sacrifice is the fourth, the sacrificial gift (dakṣiṇā) is the loose earth;
the self is the fifth, the offspring is the loose earth.
If he should pile [it] as having three layers, he would cut off the sacrifice, the sacrificial gift, the self, and offspring;
therefore it should be piled as having five layers.
In that there are three layers,
threefold indeed is Agni;
in that there are two,
two-footed is the sacrificer.
Three:
these worlds;
in these worlds indeed he prospers.

22.5.m:
In harmony the year with the Āyus,
in harmony the Dawn with the ruddy ones,
in harmony the Sun with Etaśa,
in concord the Aśvins with wonderful deeds,
in harmony Vaiśvānara with the sacrificial food,
with ghee, svāhā!
The wondrous face of the gods has arisen, the eye of Mitra, Varuṇa, and Agni; / he has filled heaven and earth and the atmosphere, the sun, the soul of what moves and stands.
Yoke, O Agni, like a charioteer, your horses that are most invoked by gods; / sit down as the ancient Hotṛ!
For yoke, O god Agni, those horses of yours that are good / and swift, that carry effectively!
You are the vehicle of Agni;
you are Agni's related vehicles;
you are the vehicle of Vāyu;
you are Vāyu's related vehicles;
you are the vehicle of the gods;
you are the gods' divine vehicles;
you are the vehicle of the atmosphere;
you are the atmosphere's related vehicles;
you are the dweller in the atmosphere;
sit in the atmosphere!
Yavas, Āyavas, Ūmas, Evas, the year, the ocean, well-measured;
for the flood, you;
for grace, you;
for truth, you;
for intention, you;
for the prudent one, you;
for heaven, you;
for light, you;
for the shining one, you;
for the Ādityas, you;
for the ṛc, you;
for brightness, you;
for brilliance, you;
for light, you;
for the sun, you;
you are the winner of the east wind;
you are the winner of cloud;
you are the winner of lightning;
you are the winner of thunder;
you are the winner of rain!
With the eastern direction, with the deity Agni, with the gāyatrī metre, I place the head of Agni;
with the gāyatrī metre, with the metre, with Agni's head, I place the head of Agni.
With the southern direction, with the deity Indra, with the triṣṭubh metre, I place the side of Agni;
with the triṣṭubh metre, with the metre, with Agni's side, I place the side of Agni.
With the western direction, with the deity Savitṛ, with the jagatī metre, I place the tail of Agni;
with the jagatī metre, with the metre, with Agni's tail, I place the tail of Agni.
With the northern direction, with the deity Mitrā-Varuṇau, with the anuṣṭubh metre, I place the side of Agni;
with the anuṣṭubh metre, with the metre, with Agni's side, I place the side of Agni.
With the upward direction, with the deity Bṛhaspati, with the paṅkti metre, I place the back of Agni;
with the paṅkti metre, with the metre, with Agni's back, he places the back of Agni.
The Gatherer (Saṃyat) and the Wise (Pracetas), of Agni, of Soma, of Sūrya—
these are their overlords;
whom we hate and who hates us, him I place in their jaws!
The Fierce (Ugrā) and the Terrible (Bhīmā), of the Fathers, of Yama, of Indra—
these are their overlords;
whom we hate and who hates us, him I place in their jaws!
The Firm (Dhruvā) and the Earth (Pṛthivī), of Savitṛ, of the Maruts, of Varuṇa—
these are their overlords;
whom we hate and who hates us, him I place in their jaws!
The Restrainer (Yantrī) and the Controller (Yamanī), of Mitrā-Varuṇau, of Mitra, of Dhātṛ—
these are their overlords;
whom we hate and who hates us, him I place in their jaws!
The Eastward (Prācī) and the Westward (Pratīcī), of the Vasus, of the Rudras, of the Ādityas—
these are their overlords;
whom we hate and who hates us, him I place in their jaws!

22.6.m:
"In harmony the year with the Āyus":
the year is the abda;
the months are the Āyus, the dawn the ruddy ones, the sun Etaśa, these [two] the Aśvins;
the year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
cattle are the sacrificial food;
cattle are ghee.
Along with the year cattle are propagated;
from the year he propagates cattle for him.
He offers in a clump of darbha grass.
Whatever un-afflicted [part], whatever vigor this [earth] has, that rises up here;
in whatever is her un-afflicted [part], whatever is her vigor, in that he offers.
He who has piled the fire does not die, for whom they offer thus.
With "The wondrous face of the gods has arisen", he offers on the human head.
Mitra is the day, Varuṇa the night;
so much indeed is [the world];
as much as there is, that he wins.
He offers with a solar verse;
therefore, among animals, man alone [stands] facing the sun; the others are bent downward.
In that he offers twice with the same verse,
therefore man, being symmetrical, has two legs.
Whoever indeed does not yoke the fire when the yoking has come, does not yoke among those who yoke.
With "Yoke, O Agni, those most invoked by gods" [and] "For yoke, O Agni, those of yours":
he yokes the fire itself when the yoking has come;
he yokes among those who yoke, who knows thus.
He yokes with two [verses];
not single [is] a pair.
Whoever indeed does not unyoke the fire when the unyoking has come, does not unyoke among those who unyoke.
With "Like a mother her son, the earth [holds] the one belonging to the loose earth":
he unyokes the fire itself when the unyoking has come;
he unyokes among those who unyoke, who knows thus.
Now these are the Saṃyānī [bricks];
these deities belong to the heavenly world;
by them indeed he goes to the heavenly world.
This [fire] is the gāyatrī with wings of light;
by it indeed he goes to the heavenly world.
Just as one travels on together by boat from water to water, even so by these the fire-piler travels on together yonder according to his world;
that is the travelling-power of the Saṃyānī [bricks].
Now these are the names of the seasons;
he wins the seasons themselves.
"Sumeka" [it says];
the year is indeed sumeka;
he wins the year itself.
Now these are the Ādityeṣṭakās [bricks of the Ādityas];
the Ādityas take away the fiery energy of him who, having piled the fire, does not seem to shine.
When these are laid down,
just as one places more upon a blazing fire, even so he places fiery energy upon it in proper sequence;
the Ādityas do not take away his fiery energy;
as that [sun] is among the gods, so he becomes among men.
Now these are the Ghṛteṣṭakās [ghee-bricks];
Agni is fiery energy, ghee is fiery energy;
in fiery energy he places fiery energy.
Now these are the Vṛṣṭisanis [rain-winning bricks];
by these he wins rain itself.
By these it rains for the fire-piler yonder according to his desire;
that is the rain-winning nature of the Vṛṣṭisanis.
Now these are the body (ātman);
Śaṅkha Kauṣya said this to his son who had piled the fire: "Did you pile the fire?"
"I piled it indeed!"
"Then how is it that you did not go with your whole body (sarvātman)?"
"For that indeed we certainly did not know!"
When these are laid down,
he piles the fire with its body (ātman);
they are pierced again;
he unites breath upon breath itself.
Now this is the sixth layer;
he who, having piled the fire, should not seem to be firmly established, he should lay down this sixth layer;
six are the seasons, the year [is sixfold];
he becomes firmly established in the seasons, in the year;
these deities belong to the heavenly world;
by them indeed he goes to the heavenly world.
These deities are suitable for sorcery (abhicāra);
whomever he hates, of him he should say: "I place that one in your jaws!";
he cuts him off from these deities;
quickly he perishes.

22.8.m:
The fire is to be piled according to its proper form.
For he who piles the fire according to its proper form, for him nothing deformed is born.
With the gāyatrī he should touch the first layer when piled;
with the triṣṭubh the second;
with the jagatī the third;
with the anuṣṭubh the fourth;
with the paṅkti the fifth.
He piles the fire according to its proper form; nothing deformed is born to him.
The first layer is the out-breath, the second the circulating breath, the third the in-breath;
with the out-breath and the in-breath he enkindles it.
This is the division of the sacrificial food (iḍā);
cattle are the sacrificial food;
with cattle he piles it.
The fire is to be piled having announced it to Prajāpati.
He who piles the fire without having announced it to Prajāpati incurs misfortune.
Two horses should stand on either side: a brown one on the north, a white one on the south.
Touching them, he should lay down each brick.
This is the form of Prajāpati;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati.
Having announced it to Prajāpati he piles the fire; he does not incur misfortune.
He gives to the Brahman priest a golden vessel filled with honey;
he becomes sweet.
[It is] of a hundred measures;
man has a hundred years of life, a hundredfold vigor;
life and vigor he secures.
With the [verse] containing the word 'wondrous' (citravat)—'The wondrous face of the gods has arisen'—he looks at it;
wondrous indeed he becomes.
At midday he causes the horse to smell [the fire];
that [sun] is the Āditya;
this Indra is Prajāpati;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati;
manifestly he prospers him.
He who knows the obtaining of the fire obtains that wish for which wish the fire is piled.
About to set forth, he should attend upon the piled fire with: 'By consecration (dīkṣā) I have obtained you; by austerity (tapas) I have obtained you; by soma-pressing (sutyā) I have obtained you; by the purificatory bath (avabhṛtha) I have obtained you; by the barren cow (vaśā) I have obtained you.'
That amounts to five;
five are the seasons;
the seasons are the year;
the year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he obtains that wish for which wish the fire is piled.
'There is only one brick,' say the theologians;
'Speech is the one brick';
'The brāhmaṇa is the one brick';
'These worlds are [the bricks].'
For this the Śarkarākṣyas indeed used to say:
'We know only three bricks as the fire to be piled: these worlds.'
The fire is to be piled endowed with truth.
With 'bhūḥ' he should touch the first naturally perforated [brick];
with 'bhuvaḥ' the second;
with 'svar' the third.
This is the truth of speech;
endowed with truth he piles the fire.
'Have you piled the fire endowed with truth or lacking truth?' say the theologians.
He indeed piles it endowed with truth who piles it thus.
'For which deity is the fire piled?' say the theologians.
'For Prajāpati alone.'
'What is his oblation?'
'The self alone.'

22.13.m:
They build the hall with beams pointing east.
The eastern direction is the direction of the gods;
he turns toward the world of the gods.
Yonder sun is in the east;
he ascends toward yonder sun.
They sacrifice for him in an enclosed [place];
having enclosed the world of the gods, they consecrate him in it.
He indeed departs from this world who goes to yonder world;
yonder world is, as it were, concealed from here.
He should make light-holes in the corners;
thereby he does not depart from this world.
He offers an eleven-potsherd cake to Agni and Viṣṇu.
Agni is all the deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
Having taken hold of both the deities and the sacrifice, he enters upon the consecration.
Agni is a purifier (pavitra), Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
purified by the purifier, he takes hold of the sacrifice.
Because it is on eight potsherds,
thereby it belongs to Agni;
because it is on three potsherds,
thereby it belongs to Viṣṇu;
thereby he does not depart from either.
He offers an eleven-potsherd cake to Agni and Viṣṇu.
Agni is the lowest of the gods, Viṣṇu the highest;
just as one binds the two ends with a tie, even so, having grasped them from both sides, he places the deities in the middle.
There should be a cake for one desiring splendour of sacred knowledge (brahmavarcasa);
the cake is the head of the sacrifice;
the cake is splendour of sacred knowledge;
he becomes possessed of splendour of sacred knowledge.
There should be a caru cooked in ghee for one desiring offspring or desiring cattle.
This is a pair: ghee and rice-grains;
ghee is the milk of the cow, rice-grains of the draft-ox;
he places a pair at the mouth of the sacrifice
for procreation.
From whatever form this caru [is],
for, vibrating as it were, the consecrated one goes to yonder world,
for he is consecrated toward yonder world;
that there is a caru
is for prosperity.
He anoints [his eyes];
he anoints [his body];
he bathes;
he puts on a garment;
these, indeed, are the bodies of man;
having become complete in body, he enters upon the consecration.
Man is defective within, impure;
that he eats
is for purity, for freedom from defects.
In this way, indeed, [one is] in yonder world as one being consecrated;
as he thinks, "Thus it will not burn me," so should he fast;
moreover, in such a way that, being consecrated, he may fast from lesser [food], so should he eat.
He shaves his hair and beard, clips his nails, cleans his teeth;
this in man is dead, impure;
having struck away the impure, he approaches the sacrificial substance.
He bathes.
The Aṅgirases, having placed the consecration in the waters, went to the heavenly world;
that he bathes,
thereby he takes hold of that consecration.
He should bathe up to the neck,
for having placed it neck-deep, they went;
with whatever amount of water he bathes, by that he takes hold of that consecration.
He should not bathe in flowing [waters];
if he should bathe in flowing [waters], dominion (kṣatra) would go to renown.
He should not bathe in well-[waters];
if he should bathe in well-[waters], the common people (viś) would go to renown.
He should bathe in standing [waters];
these among waters are consecrated, rich in cattle, which are standing;
hairy, continuous; cattle are hairy;
he bestows renown upon the sacrificer.
He should bathe at a sacred bathing-place (tīrtha);
he becomes a sacred bathing-place of his equals.

22.14.m:
The kindler of directions through food-desire, finding heaven for us, sweet, from here may the sweet one protect us;
may Agni the god, hard to cross, undeceived, quicken this holy power (brahman), may he protect us.
May the Rathaṃtara protect us with the sāmans, the Gāyatrī, all-formed among the metres;
may the Trivṛt stoma with its placement among days, the ocean, the wind, fill this strength (ojas).
The fierce, the overcoming of directions, conferring vigour, clean, pure in the day of strength;
through the overlordships of Indra may it fill us from here; may it support this great dominion on all sides.
The Bṛhat sāman, bearing dominion, of increased virility, the Triṣṭubh, strength, adorned, of fierce heroes;
O Indra, with the fifteenfold stoma, with the wind, with the ocean, protect this strength.
The eastern among directions together with food-desire for us, all the gods, finding heaven through the rainy season among days;
may this dominion be hard to cross, strength unassailable, thousandfold, mighty.
With the Vairūpa sāman may I be able to do that; with the Jagatī I introduce him into the clans;
all the gods with the seventeenfold [stoma] into the middle, this dominion, the billow, the fierce wind.
The restrainer of directions has held this dominion, the womb, food-desire, possessed of Mitra, this strength;
Mitra and Varuṇa, moving swiftly with autumn among days, may this strength be of undeceived dominion.
Upon the Vairāja sāman is my thought, gathered by the Anuṣṭubh, hard-to-overcome might;
this dominion, rich in friends, giving moisture, strength, O Mitra and Varuṇa, protect with overlordships.
The sovereign among directions, together with the sāman, mighty, may the winter season protect us through its placement;
the Bṛhatī, wishing for help, the Śakvarī like a wind, may she who faces ghee favour this sacrifice.
Possessed of light, easily milked, rich in milk, may she who faces ghee favour this sacrifice;
thou art our herdsman, helper, and restrainer; O Bṛhaspati, yoke restraining speech.
The upward among directions, the delight, the food-desire of plants, with the year, may Savitṛ [protect] us among days;
the Revat sāman and the Atichandas metre; be thou without enemies, easy to invoke for us.
In the thirty-threefold stoma, O wife of the world, in the wind shining forth, sing praise unto us;
rich in ghee under the overlordship of Savitṛ, rich in milk, may the delight, the food-desire, be ours.
"The golden germ..."
"He who breathes..."
The virāj of directions, wife of Viṣṇu, not terrible, ruling over this might, who is the recipient of desire;
all-pervading, invigorating, well-become, may Aditi be kindly to us in her lap.
The prop of heaven, supporter of the earth, ruling over this might, the wife of Viṣṇu;
may Bṛhaspati, Mātariśvan, and Vāyu, the blowing winds, sing praise to us.
"Vaiśvānara for our help..."
"Questioned in heaven..."
"Afterwards indeed, O Anumati, thou..."
"What name of thine..."

23.1.m:
The consecrated one is an oblation.
An oblation not smeared with ghee does not attain sacrificial fitness.
When he anoints him,
he sprinkles ghee upon him
for sacrificial fitness.
Ghee belongs to the gods; clarified butter [belongs] to men; half-churned butter to the gandharvas; butter melted by itself to the sun.
Fresh butter is neither among the gods nor among men.
The consecrated one is neither among the gods nor among men,
having fallen from this world [and] not having reached yonder [world].
When he anoints himself with fresh butter,
[it is] for proper fulfillment.
He puts on a garment.
The hem belongs to Agni; the waistband to the ancestors; the fringe of the border to the plants; the wind-protector to Vāyu; the warps and the woofs to the All-gods; the eyelets to the nakṣatras.
The garment belongs to all the gods; the consecrated one belongs to all the gods.
He equips him with all the deities.
He applies collyrium.
When Indra had slain Vṛtra, the demons pursued him;
he blocked them off by means of the collyrium-mountain.
The demons pursue him who is consecrated,
for so it happens.
When he applies collyrium,
[it is] to block off the demons.
Indra slew Vṛtra;
his eye flew away;
it entered Mount Trikakubh.
When he applies collyrium,
[it is] to win that very eye.
He applies [it] twice to the right [eye], once to the left.
The sacrifice is threefold;
he undertakes the sacrifice from the very beginning.
He applies [it] thrice to the right, twice to the left.
The sacrifice is fivefold;
he wins the sacrifice.
He applies [it] first to the right [eye],
for men apply [it] first to the left;
[this is] for differentiation.
He applies [it] with a yajus;
for men apply [it] without a yajus;
[this is] for differentiation.
He applies [it] moving outward;
he does not run it back;
for men apply [it] back and forth;
[this is] for differentiation.
He applies [it] with a reed-stalk;
for men apply [it] with a porcupine quill;
[this is] for differentiation.
It has a tuft;
that which has a tuft belongs to Mitra; that which is withered belongs to Varuṇa; the consecrated one belongs to Mitra;
[it is] for proper fulfillment.
Deficient, as it were, defective, as it were, is the eye;
when he applies collyrium,
he fills that very [defect];
[it is] for defectlessness.
He purifies [him].
Indra slew Vṛtra;
he slew him right upon the waters.
Those of them that were fit for sacrifice, pure,
they stepped forth;
they became the plants.
The brilliance that was theirs, that is the darbha grass;
therefore it is a purifier;
therefore with darbha blades they purify the waters.
When he purifies [him] with bunches of darbha grass,
he purifies him with the brilliance of the waters and the plants.
He purifies [him] with two [stalks].
Two are truth and untruth;
with truth alone he purifies his untruth.
Two are the in-breath and the out-breath.
He purifies [him] with three [stalks];
for the gods are thrice-truthful;
moreover, three are the breaths: the in-breath, the circulating breath, and the out-breath;
these he places in the sacrificer.
He purifies [him] with five [stalks].
Man is fivefold;
as great as his self is, so much of him he purifies.
He purifies [him] with seven [stalks].
Seven are the metres;
with the metres he purifies him.
He purifies [him] with nine [stalks].
Nine are the breaths;
with the breaths he purifies him.
He purifies [him] with twenty-one [stalks].
Man is twenty-one-fold: ten fingers on the hands, ten toes on the feet, the self is the twenty-first;
as great as his self is, so much of him he purifies.
"May the lord of thought purify you," [he says];
the lord of thought is holy power (brahman);
by holy power he purifies him.
"May the lord of speech purify you," [he says];
for he is the purifier.
"May the divine Savitṛ purify you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he purifies him with the deities.
"With an unbroken purifier," [he says];
without a flaw he purifies him.
"With the rays of the sun," [he says];
with the very body of the sun he purifies him.
"With the purifier belonging to thee, the lord of the purifier," [he says];
the lord of the purifier is the sacrifice;
for the sacrifice he purifies this.
"May I be able to achieve that for which I purify [thee]," [he says];
"May I be able to achieve the sacrifice," is what he thereby says.
He purifies him from front to back.
From front to back is the breath of man;
when the breath goes crosswise, then he perishes.
If he were to purify [him] crosswise, he would make his breath go crosswise; he would perish.
He makes his breath run along his body.
He purifies [him] outside;
having purified him in the human world, he leads him purified to the world of the gods.

23.8.m:
To the Svarga world indeed they go by that which is the introductory [sacrifice] (prāyaṇīya).
That is the introductory nature of the introductory sacrifice.
He is cut off from this world who goes to that world,
when the concluding [sacrifice] (udayanīya) belongs to Aditi (āditya).
He establishes himself firmly in this very world.
The gods, having slain the Asuras and fleeing from blood-money, confused the directions, thinking: 'Lest another should know this after [us]'.
Regarding that they say:
'The Sādhyas indeed were the gods prior to the gods.
They, having confused the directions, went to the Svarga world,
thinking: "Lest another should know this world after [us]".'
These lower gods sought to know after them;
they did not know [them].
Aditi said: 'I choose a boon. I shall proclaim these directions to you, provided that the sacrifice begins with me and concludes with me.'
Therefore the introductory sacrifice belongs to Aditi, and the concluding sacrifice belongs to Aditi,
for the boon was chosen by her.
'Sacrifice to Pathyā Svasti,' she said; 'by her you will know the eastern direction.'
'Sacrifice to Agni,' she said; 'by him the southern.'
'Sacrifice to Soma,' she said; 'by him the western.'
'Sacrifice to Savitṛ,' she said; 'by him the northern.'
'I alone know these two worlds,' she said; 'this is Aditi; hers is this upward direction.'
Upward indeed by that they went to the Svarga world.
These very deities are sacrificed to in this manner,
for the arrangement of the directions,
for the discernment of the Svarga world.
By Pathyā Svasti the gods knew the eastern direction;
Agni and Soma looked along together;
Savitṛ impelled;
this is Aditi: upon her they extended the sacrifice eastward.
That which is the introductory [sacrifice] is gathered as the opening of the sacrifice.
Speech is Pathyā Svasti.
When he sacrifices to Pathyā Svasti,
he gathers speech itself.
He gathers the out-breath and the in-breath by Agni and Soma,
Savitṛ for impelling.
This is Aditi:
upon her he gathers the opening of the sacrifice.
That which is the introductory sacrifice is offered for order.
The Maruts are the divine clan;
when the divine clan is arranged, the human clan is arranged after [it].
When there is the quarter-verse containing the Maruts in the offering-verse: 'O Maruts, grant well-being for wealth',
he arranges the divine clan itself;
when it is arranged, the human clan is arranged after [it].
He sacrifices to Pathyā Svasti;
therefore yonder sun rises in the east without interruption and sets in the west,
for he moves along Pathyā Svasti.
He sacrifices to Agni;
therefore plants ripen from the south forward as they come,
for plants belong to Agni.
He sacrifices to Soma;
therefore the waters flow mostly westward,
for the waters belong to Soma.
He sacrifices to Savitṛ;
therefore this [wind] blows mostly from the north,
for impelled by Savitṛ he blows.
He sacrifices to Aditi;
therefore she rains from above for the creatures.
He sacrifices to these five deities;
the sacrifice has five parts;
he obtains the sacrifice itself.
He cuts off a sixth [portion] for Agni;
there are six seasons;
he firmly establishes himself in the seasons themselves.
There are ten offering- and invitatory-verses;
the Virāj has ten syllables; food is the Virāj;
he firmly establishes himself in the Virāj, in food.
There are two for the Sviṣṭakṛt, [making] twelve;
the year has twelve months,
for the attainment of the year.
There are seventeen kindling-verses;
the year has seventeen parts;
five seasons, twelve months: this indeed is that year;
from the year itself he extends the opening of the sacrifice.
